bandwidth-logger (1.6.0-1) noble; urgency=medium

  * Work the next run time out from the recorded history when systemd will
    not report it. OnUnitActiveSec fires one interval after the service last
    ran, and the application already records exactly when that was, so the
    answer can be derived without asking systemd at all. It is labelled
    "about" so a calculation is never mistaken for systemd's own figure.
  * Do not offer a stale estimate: if the expected time is well past, the
    schedule was interrupted and the history is the wrong basis for a
    prediction.
  * Make the cross-process lock test deterministic by taking the lock
    directly rather than racing a running test.

 -- Bandwidth Logger contributors <bandwidth-logger@example.invalid>  Tue, 04 Aug 2026 18:00:00 +0000

bandwidth-logger (1.5.0-1) noble; urgency=medium

  * Read the next run time from "systemctl list-timers --json" rather than
    from "systemctl show". On systemd 255, with the timer active and counting
    down correctly, show reported NextElapseUSecMonotonic=infinity, omitted
    NextElapseUSecRealtime, and pretty-printed the timestamps as dates rather
    than raw microseconds -- while list-timers gave the correct next run to
    the second. The show properties remain as a fallback for older versions.
  * Never describe an active timer as "Not scheduled". When the timer is
    running but its next firing cannot be read, the window now says
    "Scheduled (next run time unavailable)", which is true, instead of
    denying that any test is queued.
  * Tolerate non-numeric systemd time values such as "infinity" instead of
    failing to parse them.
  * Make the cross-process lock test tolerate a slow interpreter start; it
    could fail under load for reasons unrelated to locking.

 -- Bandwidth Logger contributors <bandwidth-logger@example.invalid>  Tue, 04 Aug 2026 17:00:00 +0000

bandwidth-logger (1.4.0-1) noble; urgency=medium

  * Fix "Next test: Not scheduled" being shown while the timer was running
    normally. The timer is defined with OnActiveSec/OnUnitActiveSec, which
    are monotonic, so systemd reports the next firing in
    NextElapseUSecMonotonic and leaves NextElapseUSecRealtime at zero. Only
    the realtime property was read, so a healthy, actively counting timer was
    reported as not scheduled. Both are now read.
  * Reword the automatic testing control. The state word sat to the right of
    the switch, where it reads as a label for the control beside it rather
    than as the switch's own state, so the switch position could be read
    backwards. It now reads "Automatic testing is currently Off" with the
    switch after it.
  * Re-sync the switch from the stored setting on every refresh, so the
    toggle and the schedule cannot silently disagree.

 -- Bandwidth Logger contributors <bandwidth-logger@example.invalid>  Tue, 04 Aug 2026 16:00:00 +0000

bandwidth-logger (1.3.0-1) noble; urgency=medium

  * Add Delete selected. Rows in the table can now be multi-selected and
    deleted individually, with the same confirmation stating the count and
    the dates covered. Deleting a range could not remove one bad reading
    without taking its neighbours.
  * Say something when Apply is pressed with automatic testing switched off.
    It saved the interval and scheduled nothing, in silence, which was
    indistinguishable from having started the schedule.

 -- Bandwidth Logger contributors <bandwidth-logger@example.invalid>  Tue, 04 Aug 2026 15:00:00 +0000

bandwidth-logger (1.2.0-1) noble; urgency=medium

  * Add a Test server setting. The engine chooses a server by lowest latency,
    which is not the same as fastest: measured on one gigabit line, the
    lowest-latency server returned 696 Mbps while another 0.5 ms further away
    returned 926. Left automatic the engine may also choose differently
    between runs, so a change in a recorded speed could mean only that a
    different server answered -- which defeats the purpose of keeping a
    history.
  * Settings can now fetch the list of nearby servers and pin one. Fetching
    contacts Ookla, so it happens only when asked and never during a
    scheduled test.
  * A pinned server id is validated as numeric before use.

 -- Bandwidth Logger contributors <bandwidth-logger@example.invalid>  Tue, 04 Aug 2026 14:00:00 +0000

bandwidth-logger (1.1.0-1) noble; urgency=medium

  * Remove the speedtest-cli fallback engine. It under-reported throughput
    badly on fast connections -- roughly half the real figure on a gigabit
    line -- because it cannot open enough parallel connections to fill one.
    For an application whose purpose is diagnosing a slow connection, a
    number that is quietly wrong by half is worse than no number at all.
  * Conflict with speedtest-cli rather than depending on it. Ubuntu's package
    owns /usr/bin/speedtest, the same path Ookla's package installs to, so
    having it present made the Ookla engine impossible to install.
  * Correct the Ookla install commands. Ookla publishes no repository for
    Ubuntu 24.04, so the upstream script must be told to use the jammy
    repository, which runs correctly on 24.04. The commands now also remove
    the conflicting package first. Verified working end to end.
  * Annotate stored results measured by the removed engine, so an old low
    reading cannot be misread as a real slowdown. No record is rewritten or
    deleted.

 -- Bandwidth Logger contributors <bandwidth-logger@example.invalid>  Tue, 04 Aug 2026 13:00:00 +0000

bandwidth-logger (1.0.0-1) noble; urgency=medium

  * First release.
  * Manual and scheduled internet speed tests with a complete local history.
  * Every attempted test is recorded, including failures, timeouts and
    attempts skipped because another test was still running.
  * Background scheduling through a systemd user timer, so testing continues
    with the window closed and resumes after login.
  * Full and date-range CSV export, with the external IP address excluded by
    default.
  * Ookla Speedtest CLI is used when installed; the open-source speedtest-cli
    is a package dependency so the application works on first launch.

 -- Bandwidth Logger contributors <bandwidth-logger@example.invalid>  Tue, 04 Aug 2026 09:00:00 +0000
